Subject: server: build the api-key-exempt and frontend path sets from --api-prefix
Upstream issue: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/22474
                "server: --api-key doesn't recognize public endpoints when
                --api-prefix is set" — opened 2026-04-28, closed 2026-06-12 as
                `not_planned` by a STALE BOT with no maintainer response. Never
                triaged. RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-02 at the pin below: still present,
                and the surrounding code has been REFACTORED WITHOUT FIXING IT.
Applies to:     TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant @ fb2cc35ab3507a5aab56fe593620cd96d8f45384
                (tools/server/server-http.cpp)

WHAT IT FIXES
=============
`--api-prefix` and `--api-key` are each fine alone and BROKEN TOGETHER.

  * Every route is registered under the prefix — see server_http_context::get(),
    ::post(), ::del() and the Web UI block, where path_prefix = params.api_prefix.
    With `--api-prefix /llama` the health route lives at `/llama/health`.
  * The exempt sets are built from BARE literals and the middlewares test
    `.count(req.path)` — an EXACT match against the RAW WIRE PATH.
  * Those middlewares are installed with `set_pre_routing_handler`, so they run
    BEFORE routing. Nothing has stripped the prefix by then, and nothing will.

⇒ With both flags set, `/llama/health` is not in the set and returns 401. That is not
merely cosmetic here: droste's healthcheck reads 401 as unhealthy, podman restarts the
container, and the box enters a restart loop that ejects every interactive shell. It
also 401s `/llama/` and `/llama/index.html`, i.e. the user's Web UI is unusable.

🚨 THERE ARE NOW TWO SITES, NOT ONE — THE SECOND IS NEW AT THIS PIN
===================================================================
The previous pin (337f08e8) built one set inline. This pin has split the UI assets
into their own `frontend_paths`, which is consumed BOTH by `get_public_endpoints`
(the api-key exemption) AND by `middleware_server_state`. The second consumer has
the identical defect in a milder form: while the model is still loading, a prefixed
UI request is not recognised as a frontend asset and receives a 503 instead of the
loading page. Prefixing `frontend_paths` at its source fixes both consumers at once,
which is why this patch touches it rather than only the exemption set.

⭐ THE REFACTOR IS THE WARNING WORTH READING: upstream reorganised this exact code
and carried the bug across intact. Do not assume a future reflow has fixed it —
CHECK, using the guard's pre-conditions, which is what they are for.

WHY WE CARRY IT
===============
Jei's ruling: "if someone turns on the api-prefix, I don't want it to fail purely based
on that." Both settings are exposed on llama's config surface, so the pair is reachable
by a user who did nothing wrong.

🚨 THIS PATCH IS PERMANENT, NOT A STOPGAP. Upstream's own rules do not permit us to
re-file #22474 and we are not pursuing it by any other route ("Someone else might refile,
but not us."). NOBODY IS WATCHING FOR THIS TO BECOME UNNECESSARY. Treat it as load-bearing
indefinitely and expect it to survive every pin bump.

NOT A WIDENING. With a key set and no prefix, `GET /health` already returns 200 — these
endpoints are public by upstream's design. Prefixing restores parity with the no-prefix
case; it makes nothing public that was not already public. The LISTS are untouched: the
same four API paths and the same UI assets, only relocated to where they are served.

`params.api_prefix` defaults to "" (common/common.h), so `path_prefix + "/health"` is
byte-identical to "/health" on an unprefixed server. Fully backward compatible.

NEITHER SET IS `static` ANY MORE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE
========================================================
A function-local static initialises once per PROCESS and would freeze whichever prefix
it saw first — the same class of bug wearing a different hat. Both sets are therefore
ordinary locals, and both middlewares capture what they need BY VALUE: they are stored
in the server via set_pre_routing_handler and outlive init()'s stack frame, so a
reference capture would dangle.

WHAT WOULD MAKE IT REMOVABLE (a condition to NOTICE, not an outcome to expect)
==============================================================================
If a future pin ever builds these sets from the configured prefix itself, this patch
stops applying and the anchor guard in scaffolding/Container.llama-build goes red. THAT
GUARD IS THE ONLY THING THAT WILL EVER TELL US. When it fires, read the new source
before assuming anything: it may mean upstream fixed it (delete the patch and the
guard), or merely that the fork moved again and the hunks must be RE-DERIVED at the new
sha — which is what happened at this bump.

⚠️ DO NOT RE-DERIVE THIS FROM ggml-org MASTER. The fork is diverged from master (ahead
350, behind 476 as of this pin) and their exempt sets differ: master has dropped
"/models" and "/v1/models". Copying master's list would silently change WHICH endpoints
are public on our pin.
